  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">ADI provided AUD 4.5 million anchor investment into SEACEF II and engaged extensively to improve their fund offering, helping to de-risk the transaction and attract additional private and public investment. The fund reached a first close of AUD 191 million. 
&#x200d;
SEACEF II invests in companies setting up utility scale projects and early-stage businesses in Vietnam, Philippines and Indonesia. The fund targets climate change mitigation, focusing on renewable energy, energy efficiency, and clean technology innovations, and creating inclusive economic opportunities. The pipeline of investments includes companies providing open network EV charging (Eboost), rootop solar (Xurya Daya; Stride) and energy efficiency solutions.
&#x200d;
SEACEF II invests in female-led businesses, recognising their role in fostering innovation and resilience within the clean energy sector. The fund supports portfolio companies in becoming &quot;gender smart&quot; by integrating gender considerations into their operations, policies, and decision-making processes.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">ADI provides early stage and concessional investment to impact investment funds. These funds provide critical early-stage finance to small and medium businesses that are driving climate action and gender equality in the Indo-Pacific. For example, ADI is scaling up its use of early-stage and catalytic investment in climate tech businesses. In January 2024, ADI closed a $4.5 million anchor investment in Clime Capital&#x2019;s Southeast Asia Clean Energy Fund II (SEACEF II). This deal will support companies setting up utility-scale clean energy projects and early-stage climate businesses in Southeast Asia. ADI played a critical role as an early and collaborative investor, allowing SEACEF II to reach a first close in quarter four of 2023 of nearly $200 million. 
SEACEF II had its second close with a total fund size of $223 million, attracting additional private investors. Through ADI&#x2019;s anchor investment, it has so far mobilised over $62.4 million from private investors. This is the private finance mobilised and attributable to ADI&#x2019;s investment in SEACEF II using the OECD&#x2019;s Commonwealth Reporting Standard, the global standard for reporting private finance mobilised by development finance mechanisms.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">F88 Business Joint Stock Company (F88), a portfolio company of Lendable which is funded by Australian Development Investments (ADI), is a leading lender for underserved micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) and individuals in Vietnam. As of end 2024, through the support of ADI, F88 has helped approximately 4,000 women microentrepreneur borrowers in Vietnam. With 868 branches, F88 provides fast, collateral-based loans ranging from VND3 million to 2 billion (AUD181-121,000) with flexible repayment terms of 1 to 24 months. 
&#x200d;
As F88 scales, integrating a gender lens approach has become critical to fostering a more equitable and inclusive workplace and improving business performance. F88 is now embedding gender considerations into product design. In March 2024, it launched a loan product tailored for women entrepreneurs&#x2014;simplifying eligibility and targeting women aged 20 to 55 managing microbusinesses such as retail shops, food stalls, and salons.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">From inception in 2020 to the end of 2024, Australian Development Investments (ADI) mobilised AUD181.45 million in private capital, based on a total capital commitment of AUD33.30 million. This means that for every dollar invested by ADI, more than five dollars has been mobilised from the private sector.
&#x200d;
Australian Development Investments (ADI) is scaling up its use of early-stage and catalytic investment into climate-focused businesses. Following ADI&#x2019;s anchor investment of AUD4.8 million into Clime Capital&#x2019;s Southeast Asia Clean Energy Fund (SEACEF) II in 2023 and extensive support to improve their fund offering, Clime Capital successfully reached its target fundraise size of USD176.50 million in 2024. ADI&#x2019;s early participation in SEACEF II was catalytic, with Australia&apos;s investment estimated to mobilise an additional AUD74.57 million in private capital (i.e. a leverage ratio of approximately 17:1).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">ShopUp, a portfolio company of Lendable which is funded by ADI, is Bangladesh&#x2019;s leading businesses to business e-commerce platform for mom-and-pop retail shops. The company uses technology to provide businesses with easy access to wholesale goods, delivery services and credit. ShopUp has a footprint in all 64 districts of the country and has launched three products:
&#x200d;
-Mokam, a one-stop wholesale marketplace app that offers clients a lower price for stocks as ShopUp can aggregate products from a range of manufacturers;
&#x200d;
-REDX, door-to-door delivery service that reduces costs to shops by removing the need for them to go physically to the local bazaars to purchase stock; and 
&#x200d;
-Baki, a buy now, pay later (BNPL) product for small retailers, providing increased entrepreneurial and income opportunities for women (who typically have limited economic opportunity and ability to work outside of their homes)</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Technology has become an accelerator for small businesses and two of three EMIIF fund managers invest in fintech. More than 10,000 microentrepreneurs and SMEs were indirectly financed through these fintechs. The majority (96%) of fintech clients are women-owned or led. 
&#x200d;
On average, there are 66% of women employees and 51% of women leaders in fund managers supported by EMIIF.</narrative>
    </description>
